#
# Conformance case: cognition/hasskill-defeasible-evidence
# AC (ME12): gmeow:hasSkill → gmeow:knowsAbout is DEFEASIBLE evidence, not a strict
# subproperty entailment. The shipped reasoning never materializes knowsAbout from a
# skill (no such rule ships in slices/core/logic); this case is the ONLY home of the
# opt-in defeasible rule, run under an explicit StratifiedNAF contract. It derives
# gmeow:knowsAbout from gmeow:hasSkill by default, BUT is defeated when the
# competence is declared purely tacit (gmeow:competenceIsPurelyTacit) — negation as
# failure. So:
#   * dana (skilled, no defeater)  => gmeow:knowsAbout is derived (defeasible default fires)
#   * cleo (skilled, purely tacit) => gmeow:knowsAbout is NOT derived (defeater blocks it)
# This is exactly the can-do / knows-that coming apart: tacit competence without
# articulable knowledge.

@prefix logic: <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/logic/> .
@prefix gmeow: <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/> .
@prefix rdf:   <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .

# Opt-in defeasible default: a skill is evidence for knowing-about, UNLESS the
# competence is declared purely tacit. The logic:negatedBody atom is the defeater.
logic:ruleSkillDefeasiblyKnowsAbout a logic:Rule ;
    logic:provenance logic:ruleSkillDefeasiblyKnowsAbout ;
    logic:formalizationCategory logic:CategoryDefeasibleDefault ;
    logic:head [ rdf:subject "?agent" ; rdf:predicate gmeow:knowsAbout ; rdf:object "?skill" ] ;
    logic:body [ rdf:subject "?agent" ; rdf:predicate gmeow:hasSkill ; rdf:object "?skill" ] ;
    logic:negatedBody [ rdf:subject "?agent" ; rdf:predicate gmeow:competenceIsPurelyTacit ; rdf:object "?skill" ] .
